<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text">Aaron J. Li<sup id="id2.1" class="ltx_sup">1,*</sup></span>  <span id="id3" class="ltx_text">Weijia Xu<sup id="id3.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>  <span id="id4" class="ltx_text">Chandan Singh<sup id="id4.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>  <span id="id5" class="ltx_text">Zixiao Chen<sup id="id5.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup></span>  <span id="id6" class="ltx_text">Jeevana Priya Inala<sup id="id6.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>  <span id="id7" class="ltx_text">Charlotte Siska<sup id="id7.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup></span>  <span id="id8" class="ltx_text">Acar Tamersoy<sup id="id8.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup></span>  <span id="id9" class="ltx_text">Bin Yu<sup id="id9.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup></span>  <span id="id10" class="ltx_text">Ion Stoica<sup id="id10.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup></span>  <span id="id11" class="ltx_text">Michel Galley<sup id="id11.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>  <span id="id12" class="ltx_text">Jianfeng Gao<sup id="id12.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Advances in frontier coding agents have sharply increased the token costs of AI-assisted software engineering. Their token efficiency is typically evaluated on isolated tasks, whereas real-world developers use them to continuously build, update, and maintain large-scale projects. This mismatch motivates us to evaluate agents over sequences of realistic coding tasks, where we find that agents exhibit recurring environment-dependent token costs that existing methods struggle to reduce. To minimize these costs, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>, an external controller that cuts environment-specific token costs over a sequence of tasks. These cuts come from determining (i) which operational micro-skills to inject, (ii) which persistently irrelevant tools to prune, and (iii) when to intervene in unproductive failure loops. Across Claude Code, Codex, and GitHub Copilot CLI paired with frontier models, <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> reduces the average token cost by 18.4–30.9% with comparable task success. Savings hold in every repository under every tested agent–model setting and generalize to diverse domains such as sequential web development and security auditing, where costs fall by 6.4–21.2% depending on task structure. Our work establishes adaptive black-box cost control as a promising direction for reducing token costs of coding agents in realistic settings.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Frontier large language models (LLMs), coupled with agent harnesses that support tool use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, have become increasingly capable of completing realistic, long-horizon software engineering tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. These components are commonly packaged as commercial products, including <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Claude Code</a>, <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Codex</a>, and <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">GitHub Copilot CLI</a>. These agents are designed primarily for generic task success, but their increased capability comes at a substantial token cost. As shown in <a href="#S1.F1.sf1" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>, the billed context at each turn includes persistent system and user instructions together with an expanding history of model outputs and tool interactions, causing earlier content to be charged repeatedly across the trajectory.</p>
</div>
