<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Trajectory anatomy and the cost–quality tradeoff. (a) Schematic of how the persistent prefix and interaction history accumulate across agent turns. (b) Cost and Pass@1 change of each method relative to high-effort default execution, one point per method in each of the five agent–model settings; the dashed line marks the Pareto frontier.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Token efficiency has primarily been studied and optimized at the level of individual tasks. Model-level controls such as lower reasoning effort save tokens at a cost in task success (<a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.2 SWE-Bench Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), while harness-level controls such as tool availability and auxiliary instructions typically require manual configuration. More targeted approaches to trajectory compression, observation filtering, and specialized repository exploration often require agent-loop modifications or additional trained components <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kovács, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, limiting their plug-and-play deployment. More importantly, task-local optimization overlooks the continuity of software development, where successive changes occur within an evolving codebase <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joshi et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Such continuity creates an opportunity for cost control: within a shared environment, such as a code repository, completed executions can provide reusable experience for future tasks. We therefore optimize cumulative cost across a sequence of tasks in that environment, rather than treating each execution in isolation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We first test whether fixed, externally applied cost-control strategies can deliver consistent token savings. Our cost profiling covers 60 SWE-Bench tasks across 6 repositories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and evaluates three representative fixed strategies from popular open-source plugins: command-output compression with <a href="https://github.com/rtk-ai/rtk" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Rust Token Killer</a> (RTK), semantic code navigation with <a href="https://github.com/oraios/serena" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Serena</a>, and structural context injection with <a href="https://aider.chat/docs/repomap.html" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Aider’s repository map</a>. Empirically, we find that no fixed strategy reduces cost consistently: each has both positive and negative repository-level effects, and their pooled effects are small (<a href="#S2.F2.fig1" title="In 2.1 Repository-specific Effects of Fixed Strategies ‣ 2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Meanwhile, the default traces reveal three recurring sources of headroom: operational knowledge is repeatedly rediscovered, most loaded tools remain unused, and failed trajectories consume disproportionate cost. Taken together, these findings point to strategies that adapt to each environment from its accumulated traces, rather than fixed strategies applied uniformly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by these findings, we introduce <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>, an experience-driven external controller for coding agents. It treats each agent as a black box: it acts through exposed prompt and tool settings, monitors execution events, and does not modify model weights or agent-loop code. At its core, a controller applies general cost-control meta-strategies to continually refine environment-specific policies from completed execution traces. These policies adaptively determine (i) which one-line operational facts about the environment to activate as micro-skills, (ii) which loaded but irrelevant tools to prune, and (iii) when to intervene in unproductive failure loops. We evaluate <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> with Claude Code, Codex, and GitHub Copilot CLI, paired with frontier models, on 277 SWE-Bench tasks across 6 repositories, 102 Web-Bench tasks across 5 web-development projects, and 200 tasks from two security-auditing benchmarks. Including all controller overhead, our method reduces mean per-task cost on SWE-Bench by 18.4–30.9%, while improving Pass@1 over default execution in 4 of 5 agent–model settings. Cost savings hold in every repository under each tested agent–model setting (<a href="#S4.F5.sf1" title="In Fig. 5 ‣ 4.2 SWE-Bench Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a>), and <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> variants account for every point on the cost–quality Pareto frontier at or above the default pass rate (<a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>). On Web-Bench and the security benchmarks, it reduces cost by 16.0–21.2% and 6.4–19.6%, respectively, with Pass@2 and detection F1 remaining comparable to default execution. Our results show that our external cost-control framework can deliver consistent and transferable cost reductions across distinct agent harnesses while maintaining comparable task quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions can be summarized as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:8mm;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate coding-agent cost optimization over sequences of tasks and define a standardized protocol for cumulative end-to-end accounting. Our cost profiling identifies repository-dependent effects of fixed strategies and three actionable sources of headroom.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>, an external controller that learns environment-specific policies for micro-skill activation, tool pruning, and failure-loop intervention.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> across three popular coding agents and four benchmarks spanning 579 tasks, demonstrating cost reductions that hold in every setting we measure, with comparable task success.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a retrospective profiling analysis on 60 SWE-Bench tasks from the verified split, with 10 tasks from each of 6 repositories, to test whether fixed strategies save cost consistently and whether completed traces reveal opportunities for adaptive control. As <a href="#S1.F1.sf1" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> illustrates, every turn bills a persistent system-and-tool prefix together with the accumulated interaction history; caching discounts this replayed context but does not eliminate its cost. <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Cost Profiling Protocol and Detailed Results ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> provides the detailed protocol.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Repository-specific Effects of Fixed Strategies</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the agent’s default execution with three fixed strategies, using the same configuration in every repository: (1) <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rust Token Killer (RTK)</span> rewrites shell commands to compress their outputs;
(2) <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Serena</span> adds a semantic code-navigation interface; and (3) Aider’s <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RepoMap</span> injects a structural map of repository symbols. Although their outputs may depend on the current task, none learns from completed executions or activates selectively based on prior experience.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:158.7pt;">
<div id="S2.F2.fig1.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.22732v1/fig_pilot_strategies.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/169;" width="238" height="169"></object>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.fig1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.fig1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Per-repository cache-aware cost change of the three fixed strategies relative to default execution on the 60 profiled tasks (negative is cheaper). Every strategy lowers cost in some repositories and raises it in others.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.F2.fig1" title="In 2.1 Repository-specific Effects of Fixed Strategies ‣ 2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that every strategy produces mixed repository-level cost changes. For example, all three reduce cost on Django but increase it on SymPy; on scikit-learn only RTK helps, while on Sphinx only RepoMap does. These mixed effects motivate learning repository-specific policies from completed tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Three Sources of Removable Cost</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.F3" title="In Operational work repeats within a repository. ‣ 2.2 Three Sources of Removable Cost ‣ 2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> identifies three potential sources of cost reduction in traces based on unmodified agent runs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Operational work repeats within a repository.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Actions that recur in at least half of a repository’s tasks account for 12–28% of the bytes its tool calls send and receive (<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Cost Profiling Protocol and Detailed Results ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> describes how calls are matched to actions). The fraction of calls matching an action from an earlier task rises from 14% for the second and third tasks to 30% once at least 5 tasks precede the current one (<a href="#S2.F3.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ Operational work repeats within a repository. ‣ 2.2 Three Sources of Removable Cost ‣ 2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a>). Against another repository’s complete history, only 7% of calls match, primarily through generic setup and testing commands. This motivates learning repository-specific operational knowledge and injecting it only when relevant.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
