</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Meta-strategy I: Experience-grounded Micro-skills</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proposal and evidence collection.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The controller periodically condenses a bounded window of completed trajectories into compact digests of operational evidence, such as command templates, test invocations, edited file names, and encountered errors. From these digests, an LLM <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proposer</em> drafts a few candidate micro-skills; its tokens, like those of the controller’s other LLM calls, are charged to the task that triggers the call. Each candidate <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> pairs a simple operational instruction with executable detectors, which are regular expressions or built-in predicates that test the skill against the actual task trajectory. After each task <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the controller runs these detectors on the shell commands and outputs in its trajectory <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. A <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scope</em> detector checks whether <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exercises</em> the skill, that is, whether the situation it addresses arose at all; a skill on reproducing issues, for example, is exercised only if the agent attempts to write a reproduction script. An exercised task <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">contradicts</em> the skill if it shows the instruction was wrong, such as an import error for a package the skill says is installed, and otherwise <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">confirms</em> it. A <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">need</em> detector records whether the task needed the skill: the agent either incurred the targeted waste, such as an unnecessary reinstall, or used the stated fact, such as a native test runner; without a need detector, every exercised task counts as needed. These records enter <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and decide which skills are activated or retired (examples in <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I Examples of Meta-strategy Decisions ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Activation and retirement.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A candidate is activated, entering <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, only while two conditions hold. It must have been confirmed on several tasks whose edited file sets do not overlap, so that its evidence reflects repository-level rather than issue-local recurrence. Its need rate, the fraction of exercising trajectories that needed it, must also stay at or above a threshold. The first condition asks whether a skill is reliable, the second whether repeatedly presenting it is worthwhile. The support of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> at task <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}(f)=\sum_{j&lt;i}\gamma^{\,i-j}\Bigl(\bm{1}[\xi_{j}\ \text{confirms}\ f]-\lambda\,\bm{1}[\xi_{j}\ \text{contradicts}\ f]\Bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>confirms</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>contradicts</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}(f)=\sum_{j&lt;i}\gamma^{\,i-j}\Bigl(\bm{1}[\xi_{j}\ \text{confirms}\ f]-\lambda\,\bm{1}[\xi_{j}\ \text{contradicts}\ f]\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{1}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{1}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator, the decay <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> discounts older evidence, and the weight <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> lets one contradiction outweigh one confirmation. A skill that has met the confirmation condition retires once <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}(f)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}(f)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and its identifier is never activated again. The asymmetry is deliberate, because the two errors are not symmetric in cost: a skill that should have been retired is re-billed on every later prefix, whereas a skill retired too eagerly can be proposed again under a new identifier. A skill thus moves from candidate to confirmed to retired (<a href="#A6.T5" title="In Micro-skill injection. ‣ Appendix F Additional Meta-strategy Details ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>); a confirmed skill below the need threshold is withheld from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> but not retired.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Meta-strategy II: Tool Pruning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tool pruning reduces the recurring cost of the agent’s tool interface by omitting schemas unlikely to help with the current task. The controller always retains core tools for shell execution and file reading, writing, and editing. Other tools are removed after a fixed number of consecutive completed tasks have left them uncalled. In addition, an LLM judge assesses the relevance of each never-called tool not yet removed, using the tool’s description and the description of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, in which links to the repository’s pull requests, issues, commits, and files are masked. A tool is removed when it meets the unused-history criterion or the judge deems it irrelevant; the remaining tools form <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and are loaded for task <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. To recover from mistaken exclusions, the controller periodically restores the full tool catalog for a re-audit. A tool called during a re-audit is never masked again, since only never-called tools are masked.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Meta-strategy III: Failure-loop Intervention</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The controller also derives a repository-specific turn budget from prior trajectories. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\xi_{j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\xi_{j}|</annotation></semantics></math> denote the number of turns in trajectory <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> indicate whether task <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> succeeded. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the empirical <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-th percentile of the turn counts of successful prior tasks. The budget for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B_{i}=\beta\cdot Q_{p}\bigl(\{|\xi_{j}|:j&lt;i,\ y_{j}=1\}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}=\beta\cdot Q_{p}\bigl(\{|\xi_{j}|:j&lt;i,\ y_{j}=1\}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> high and a margin <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> comes from the benchmark grader, but in realistic settings any comparable success signal can be used. The budget is a reference for identifying unusually long trajectories, not by itself a stopping point. After each tool call, a rule-based check looks for repeated failure signatures, dense error output, or a trajectory that has exceeded <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, without making a model call. When triggered, an LLM judge reviews a compact digest of recent actions and running counters, including commands, edited file names, and error signatures, to distinguish an unproductive loop from a difficult task that is still progressing. A confident stuck verdict first redirects the agent with a summary of its recent actions and a request to try a different approach. If the failure pattern recurs and the judge again finds the agent stuck, the controller terminates the rollout. Productive or uncertain verdicts let it continue past <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, up to the forced stop below. As a backstop that the judge cannot veto, a forced stop ends the rollout once it reaches <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> turns, for a fixed <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, provided a strict stall rule holds: edit churn, dense errors, and a repeated failure signature at once (<a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Additional Meta-strategy Details ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Controller Configuration ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> gives the hyperparameter values, and <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Additional Meta-strategy Details ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> the implementation details.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> with default execution, reduced reasoning effort, and fixed strategies on cost and task success across repositories and agent–model settings, then test transfer to web development and security auditing.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Agents, benchmarks, and methods.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate five agent–model settings: Claude Code with Sonnet 5 and with Opus 4.8, Copilot CLI with Sonnet 5, and Codex with GPT-5.6 Terra and GPT-5.6 Sol. Every setting runs the same 277 SWE-Bench tasks from 6 repositories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: scikit-learn (60), SymPy (60), Django (40), Sphinx (44), Matplotlib (34), and xarray (39). These tasks span both the verified split and harder tasks from the full split. Within a repository, tasks run in chronological order, so the controller only learns from work that came earlier in that repository’s history. Three additional benchmarks assess generalizability to other domains (<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Generalization to Diverse Task Structures ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>): 102 sequential Web-Bench tasks from 5 projects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and 100 security tasks each from <a href="https://github.com/Samsung/CredData" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">CredData</a> (credential detection) and ReposVul (vulnerability detection) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Task and Stream Selection ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> describes how we selected the tasks and streams for each benchmark. In every setting, default execution is the unmodified agent at high reasoning effort, with no external control. We compare it against agent-native execution at medium and low effort, the two fixed strategies RTK and RepoMap, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> combined with RTK or RepoMap. Serena is left out because tool pruning could remove its unused server and so confound the comparison (<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Cost Profiling Protocol and Detailed Results ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>); it was also the only fixed strategy that raised pooled cost in cost profiling (<a href="#S2.F2.fig1" title="In 2.1 Repository-specific Effects of Fixed Strategies ‣ 2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation metrics.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our quality metric is Pass@1 under the official grader on SWE-Bench, cumulative Pass@2 on Web-Bench, as recommended by the original benchmark, and detection F1 on the security benchmarks (more details are included in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Task and Stream Selection ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>); efficiency is mean cache-aware cost (<a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Task Stream Formulation and Cost Accounting ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>, <a href="#A5.E4" title="In Cost attribution and objective. ‣ Appendix E Task Stream Formulation and Cost Accounting ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), which charges every auxiliary controller call to the task that triggered it. <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Controller Configuration ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> lists the controller configuration shared by all benchmarks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>SWE-Bench Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.2 SWE-Bench Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> alone reduces mean cache-aware cost by 18.4–30.9% across all five settings. Pass@1 improves in four settings and only falls by 1.0 percentage point on Opus 4.8. Lowered reasoning effort instead reduces Pass@1 by 1.8–10.3 percentage points, and RTK or RepoMap alone saves inconsistently. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> can also be combined with either: both combinations reduce cost in all five settings, and on Claude Code with Opus 4.8, adding RTK raises the saving from 30.9% to 33.2% with Pass@1 above default.</p>
</div>
