<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The savings are consistent.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pooled savings can conceal cost increases in individual environments, as the fixed strategies in <a href="#S2" title="2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrate. <a href="#S4.F5.sf1" title="In Fig. 5 ‣ 4.2 SWE-Bench Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a> reports cost changes for each of the six repositories under each of the five agent–model settings, yielding 30 repository–setting cells. <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> reduces cost in all 30, by 5.2–40.1%, as does <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>+RTK. <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>+RepoMap reduces cost in 29 of 30. By comparison, RTK alone increases cost in 12 cells and RepoMap alone in 15.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptation expands the Pareto frontier.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> places each method’s cost and Pass@1 change relative to default execution in its agent–model setting. Reduced reasoning effort achieves large savings with substantial losses in Pass@1, while RTK and RepoMap stay closer to default and move in mixed directions. <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> variants account for every point on the Pareto frontier that maintains or improves the default pass rate, showing that adaptive control reaches a more favorable cost–quality tradeoff across settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Savings accumulate over the task stream.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> begins each repository without prior traces, so its benefit may take several tasks to emerge. <a href="#S4.F5.sf2" title="In Fig. 5 ‣ 4.2 SWE-Bench Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a> plots the cumulative cost change of <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> (without integrations) relative to default execution within each repository, averaged over the two Claude Code settings. All repositories except xarray initially cost more under <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>, but all become cheaper by the sixth task and remain cheaper thereafter, ending with cumulative savings of 16.9–32.0%.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ablations.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We run each meta-strategy alone. In the two Claude Code settings, tool pruning carries the largest single share, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-13.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>13.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-13.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-17.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>17.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-17.7\%</annotation></semantics></math>, because it acts on every turn of every task, with micro-skill injection close behind at <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-11.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>11.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-11.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-14.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>14.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-14.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> and the failure-loop guard in single digits. Pass@1 stays within 3 points of default for every variant, so none trades task success for cost, and none alone reproduces the full stack’s saving (<a href="#S4.T2.st1" title="In Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Generalization to Diverse Task Structures ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a>; activity counts in <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Ablations of Meta-strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Generalization to Diverse Task Structures</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess generalization beyond SWE-Bench, we evaluate <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> on sequential web development (Web-Bench) and two security-auditing benchmarks (CredData and ReposVul). Web-Bench typically involves smaller codebases and workspaces than SWE-Bench, but its tasks remain within one continuous project and naturally inherit earlier workspace changes. In the security benchmarks, each task targets a different repository under a fixed audit prompt, so the procedure recurs without repository continuity. Because no repository recurs, we disable repository-specific micro-skill injection, leaving only two meta-strategies active.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.T2.st2" title="In Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Generalization to Diverse Task Structures ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a> shows cost reductions of 16.0–21.2% on Web-Bench and 16.5–19.6% on CredData. The latter result shows that the remaining meta-strategies can still achieve substantial savings across new repositories under a shared audit procedure. Reductions are much smaller on ReposVul, at 6.4–9.3%, where shorter trajectories leave less removable cost. The savings thus generalize across these workloads, while their magnitude varies with the reusable structure and cost-reduction headroom within the trajectories.</p>
</div>
