<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cost reduction for LLMs and coding agents.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A classic line of work lowers inference cost by choosing which model answers each query: cascades escalate from cheaper to stronger models until an answer is judged reliable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aggarwal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and routers predict the cheapest adequate model before generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. These methods trade answer quality for cost on each query, whereas <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> keeps the model and effort fixed and removes cost that recurs within an environment. For coding agents, existing methods reduce cost within a trajectory by controlling context, interfaces, or rollout length. Context-control methods compress prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, shell output with RTK, tool observations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kovács, 2026</a>)</cite>, or accumulated trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; others narrow retrieval through repository maps, knowledge graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vogel et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, structure-aware scouting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, or trained retrieval agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bashir et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Interface-level methods restrict or retrieve candidate tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paramanayakam et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, enrich tool interfaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or encourage minimal implementations through plugins such as <a href="https://github.com/dietrichgebert/ponytail" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Ponytail</a>. Runtime methods provide corrective feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, early-exit checks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, budget estimates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, or hidden-state failure detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Methods deployable through an agent’s exposed interfaces are typically fixed and task-local; our cost profiling (<a href="#S2" title="2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) finds that their effects vary across repositories. <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> instead uses completed traces to adapt the instruction prefix, tool set, and stopping policy to each environment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-task experience for coding agents.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent benchmarks have started to evaluate agents across continual repair, related-task context reuse, and streaming task sequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joshi et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Prior methods reuse completed executions as verbal lessons or workflows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, or derive repository knowledge from commit histories and repair trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. A complementary line of work packages experience into reusable capabilities, including executable skill and tool libraries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, verified programmatic skills <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, refined skill APIs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and evolving test-time libraries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Recent systems further automate skill construction and evolution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>. Because indiscriminate repository context can increase cost without generally improving success <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gloaguen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, such experience must be reused selectively. Building on this principle, <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> uses cross-task experience to learn cost-control policies applied through agents’ exposed interfaces.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Limitations and Conclusion</h2>

<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each meta-strategy can still make an incorrect decision: a micro-skill may become stale, a pruned tool may become necessary, and a difficult but productive task may be mistaken for a failure loop. Each decision, however, comes with a recovery path that limits the damage: a stale skill is retired once a trace contradicts it, a wrongly pruned tool returns at the next re-audit, and the failure-loop intervention redirects before it stops and spares uncertain cases. Our experiments cover three widely used coding agents but not more minimal harnesses such as <a href="https://github.com/earendil-works/pi" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Pi</a>; since <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> relies only on the prompt, tool configuration, and event stream, supporting such a harness requires an adapter rather than new meta-strategies. Finally, our SWE-Bench streams consist of related but independent fixes rather than actual user sessions. Continuous sessions should offer more reusable experience (<a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Extended Discussion ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>), so interactive coding workflows are a natural next test.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conclusion.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Coding-agent costs accumulate across tasks in recurring environments, which isolated-task evaluations overlook. <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> shows that an external controller can learn from completed work to make an unchanged agent cheaper while largely preserving task success, across software engineering, web development, and security auditing.</p>
</div>
</section>
