<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Extended Discussion</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> as one instance of a broader class.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> occupies one point in a larger design space: external controllers that apply fixed, environment-agnostic meta-strategies and let completed traces set environment-specific policies. Each of our meta-strategies follows the same pattern, pairing a cheap signal from traces with a conservative action and a way to recover from mistakes: micro-skills are confirmed by detectors and retired on contradiction, pruned tools return through re-audits, and the failure-loop guard redirects before it stops. The same pattern applies to other controls that harnesses expose, such as choosing the reasoning effort or model for each task, compacting context at learned points, or reusing environment setup across tasks, and each strategy could be specialized further to a deployment, for example with longer histories, micro-skills shared across related repositories, or success signals from users rather than benchmark graders. Because the controller needs no model access, retraining, or change to the agent, it can follow agents as they evolve. We therefore see our results as evidence that this direction is promising and underexplored, not as its ceiling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generalization without per-environment tuning.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A single configuration (<a href="#A3.T4" title="In Appendix C Controller Configuration ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) serves three harnesses, five agent–model settings, and four benchmarks; the only benchmark-specific changes follow from the structure of the streams (<a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Controller Configuration ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>). That the savings hold in all 30 SWE-Bench repository–setting cells without tuning suggests that the meta-strategies capture regularities of agentic work, namely recurring operational facts, unused interfaces, and unproductive loops, rather than artifacts of one agent or dataset. The shared configuration is a default rather than a constraint, and its parameters also serve as knobs with a predictable direction: fewer required confirmations or a lower need gate activate micro-skills sooner, a shorter unused streak or a longer re-audit period keeps more tools pruned, and a smaller budget margin <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, a lower judge confidence threshold, or a smaller <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> lets the guard intervene earlier. Each adjustment trades additional savings against a higher risk of a wrong decision, so users can move along the cost–quality frontier to suit their priorities, much as with reasoning effort, whose savings come with larger losses in Pass@1 (<a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>). SWE-Bench is also a conservative test of experience reuse. Consecutive tasks are independent: each starts from a fresh checkout of its own base commit, none inherits the agent’s earlier edits, and each stream spans 1.9 to 8.5 years of its repository’s history, over which layouts, test commands, and dependencies drift. A developer’s session instead returns to a codebase that changes incrementally, often through the agent’s own work, so more of what one task teaches should remain valid for the next. Web-Bench, whose tasks inherit the workspace, is closer to that setting, and <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> saves 16.0–21.2% there even though its streams have only 20 or 21 tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Complementarity with fixed strategies.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Alone, RTK and RepoMap change Pass@1 inconsistently, by <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-3.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-3.8</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1.9</annotation></semantics></math> points across settings (<a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.2 SWE-Bench Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Their combinations with <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> are more informative. On Claude Code with Opus 4.8, where <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> alone is 1.0 point below default, adding RepoMap or RTK raises Pass@1 to 75.1 and 74.8, above default, and a combination attains the highest Pass@1 in three of the five settings. A plausible explanation is that the strategies supply different information. Micro-skills state how to operate in a repository, such as how to run its tests, but by design say nothing about where a fix belongs; RepoMap’s ranked signatures address that localization question, and RTK’s condensed logs keep failure messages salient in a shorter context. The savings, however, do not simply add up: RepoMap bills its map on every turn, so <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>+RepoMap saves less than <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span> alone in four of five settings, and RTK, whose own savings are inconsistent, adds to <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>’s saving on Opus 4.8 but lowers it by up to 5.2 points elsewhere. Combining fixed and adaptive strategies thus trades some savings for quality, and which combination is preferable depends on the setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why micro-skills.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We call the injected knowledge micro-skills to distinguish it from two forms of reusable experience in prior work (<a href="#S5" title="5 Related Work ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Skill and tool libraries package executable code, programs, or APIs for recurring subtasks, while lessons, workflows, and repository memories distill past trajectories into natural-language guidance on how to solve tasks. A micro-skill is narrower than either: a single operational fact about one environment, such as which test runner works, where sources live, or which setup step is unnecessary. Three properties make this granularity suitable for cost control. First, it targets the cost we observe: frontier agents already solve most tasks, and much of their removable spend goes to rediscovering the same environment facts from task to task (<a href="#S2" title="2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) rather than to missing problem-solving ability. Second, it is continually verifiable: detectors confirm or contradict a one-line claim about commands and outputs on every later trace without a model call, so its validity is rechecked throughout the stream rather than once when it is created, whereas whether a general lesson or procedure helped is hard to read from a trace. Third, it is cheap and low-risk: the manual is billed on every turn, so each entry must be short and earn its place, and because a micro-skill states facts rather than solutions, it carries no task-specific content from earlier tasks into later ones, and a wrong one is retired after little evidence, as Example (c) in <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I Examples of Meta-strategy Decisions ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> shows.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
