<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">External cost-saving strategies.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:5mm;">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">RTK</em> (v0.45.0) is registered as a pre-execution hook on the agent’s shell tool. It rewrites each shell command to run through RTK, which condenses the command’s output, such as test logs, listings, and diffs, before the agent sees it. The agent’s tools and prompt are unchanged.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Serena</em> is an MCP server backed by language servers. It adds symbol-level navigation tools, such as finding a symbol’s definition, listing a file’s symbols, and finding references. The agent is instructed to prefer these tools over its native file reading and search, which remain available.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">RepoMap</em> refers to Aider’s repository map. It parses the repository’s Python source, ranks files and symbols by their centrality in the cross-reference graph, biased toward identifiers mentioned in the task description, and renders the top-ranked signatures within a 2,048-token budget. The map is generated once per task and supplied as project instructions, so it becomes part of the prefix billed on every turn.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main experiments use the same RTK version and map budget, and also test <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>+RTK and <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>+RepoMap to determine whether adaptive savings complement output compression and static repository context. There is no <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TokenEconomizer</span>+Serena variant, because tool pruning may remove an unused Serena server and so directly confound the two methods; for the same reason, Serena is absent from <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.2 SWE-Bench Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Headroom measurements.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All three measurements read the default-execution traces and estimate exposure to removable cost, which should be interpreted as an upper bound rather than achievable savings.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:5mm;">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Repeated actions</em> (<a href="#S2.F3.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ Operational work repeats within a repository. ‣ 2.2 Three Sources of Removable Cost ‣ 2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a>). We reduce each tool call to an action key. For shell commands, the key is the command lower-cased, with whitespace collapsed, any leading directory change removed, and only the first 6 tokens kept. For file tools, the key is the tool name and the file’s base name. Within each repository, tasks are taken in chronological order, and for each task we measure the share of its tool calls whose key already appeared in an earlier task of the same repository. The cross-task control measures the same share against the complete history of each other repository, averaged over the five.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Unused tools</em> (<a href="#S2.F3.sf2" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ Operational work repeats within a repository. ‣ 2.2 Three Sources of Removable Cost ‣ 2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a>). We compare the tool catalog the agent receives at session start, which has 23 tools and is the same in every repository, with the tool calls it actually makes, counted per repository.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Failed tasks</em> (<a href="#S2.F3.sf3" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ Operational work repeats within a repository. ‣ 2.2 Three Sources of Removable Cost ‣ 2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(c)</span></a>). We measure the share of default spend that goes to tasks that fail, and the mean turn count of passed and failed tasks. For the spend after a trajectory first repeats a failure signature, each run’s cost is spread evenly over its tool calls, which understates late spend, since every turn re-bills the context accumulated before it.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Controller Configuration</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A3.T4" title="In Appendix C Controller Configuration ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> lists the controller configuration, and <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J Harness-specific Customization ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a> describes how each harness implements it. Within a benchmark, every repository and project uses the same values, and none is tuned per repository, project, or stream. All values were fixed before the main runs, in earlier development runs on the same six SWE-Bench repositories. These values serve six SWE-Bench repositories whose streams range from 34 to 60 tasks and whose default pass rates, turn counts, and tool usage differ widely, as well as five Web-Bench projects that span Web standards and frameworks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The settings differ across benchmarks only where the structure of the stream requires it. Web-Bench streams have 20 or 21 tasks, too few for the percentile of passed turn counts to warm up before most of the stream has run. The failure-loop guard therefore starts from a pre-registered budget of 30 turns until 5 tasks have passed. On the security benchmarks every task is a different repository, so a repository-specific micro-skill never has a later task in the same repository to apply to; micro-skill injection is disabled. Their tasks are also read-only audits under one fixed brief, so the unused-tool history reflects the audit procedure rather than any single repository, and the judge assesses each candidate against that brief.</p>
</div>
