<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Task and Stream Selection</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All methods and seeds run the same tasks in the same order, so seeds differ only in the agent’s own nondeterminism.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SWE-Bench.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the six repositories profiled in <a href="#S2" title="2 Cost Profiling and Analysis of Fixed Cost-control Strategies ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, all among the largest in SWE-Bench Verified. Of the 277 tasks, 199 come from SWE-Bench Verified and 78 from the full SWE-Bench test split:</p>
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:5mm;">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Django</em> (40), <em id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Matplotlib</em> (34), and <em id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sphinx</em> (44) use only Verified tasks. Matplotlib and Sphinx use every Verified task; Django uses 40 of its 231 Verified tasks, all from the start of its Verified history (November 2016 to September 2019), so that consecutive tasks are typically days apart (median 5 days between issue dates) rather than spread over the full 6.7 years.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scikit-learn</em> (60) combines 31 Verified tasks with 29 from the full split.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SymPy</em> (60) combines 41 Verified tasks with 19 from the full split.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">xarray</em> (39) has only 22 Verified tasks, and the 13 oldest are spread thinly over 2019 to 2021. Its stream instead uses the 9 Verified tasks from April 2022 onward, interleaved in base-commit order with 30 full-split tasks, one from February 2021 and the rest from March 2022 to January 2023, so that consecutive tasks are typically days apart (median 4 days between issue dates).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We draw the extra scikit-learn and SymPy tasks from the full split to raise the difficulty of the two streams. With Verified tasks alone, nearly every method passes nearly every task in these repositories, so default execution cannot be told apart from its reduced-effort variants. The added tasks have gold patches of 61–200 changed lines and a package version that already appears in the stream; we sort candidates by patch size and sample at even spacing. Because the full split was not validated by human annotators, we checked each added task by applying its gold patch in the official image and running the official evaluation, and every one passed. Each repository’s stream runs in base-commit order. Because fix commits are public, every method runs behind an answer-key guard that blocks direct access to the gold patch on GitHub (<a href="#A10.SS0.SSS0.Px1" title="Answer-key guard. ‣ Appendix J Harness-specific Customization ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Web-Bench.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Web-Bench consists of 50 projects, each a sequence of 20 dependent tasks, and some projects open with an extra <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">init</span> task that sets up the scaffold. We run five projects as streams in the benchmark’s own task order: calculator (21 tasks), table (21), expressjs (20), react (20), and zustand (20), for 102 tasks in total. Each setting runs every stream with three seeds. We chose the five for diversity across the benchmark’s two halves, Web standards and Web frameworks. Calculator and table are built on plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, the latter around the HTML <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;table&gt;</span> element. React is a front-end UI framework, zustand a state-management library used with React, and expressjs a Node.js back end with a database. We also excluded projects on which default execution fails early: the official protocol stops a stream at the first task that fails both attempts, so such a project would leave too short a stream to compare methods on.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We follow the official Web-Bench evaluation protocol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which allows each task two attempts. If the first attempt fails the project’s tests, the agent retries the same task with the test errors as context. The retry matters because each task builds on the previous one: without it, a single failed attempt would end the stream and leave every later task unevaluated. We therefore report Pass@2, the fraction of tasks passed on either attempt, and our cost figures include both attempts. As on SWE-Bench, every method runs behind the answer-key guard (<a href="#A10.SS0.SSS0.Px1" title="Answer-key guard. ‣ Appendix J Harness-specific Customization ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>), because the grading specifications are public.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">CredData.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CredData contains 333 repositories. We exclude the 19 that contain no true secret line: for those, predicting nothing scores an F1 of 1, so they measure only precision. We split the remaining 314 into quartiles by the number of true secret lines per repository, which is heavily skewed (cut points 4, 8.5, and 24.75), allocate the 100 slots in proportion (28, 22, 25, and 25), and select that many repositories from each quartile. The selected repositories contain between 1 and 799 true secret lines (median 8.5).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ReposVul.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the 1,310 instances of the Python split, we drop the 298 whose fix removes no line and so leaves nothing to detect, and the 109 that ReposVul flags as outdated. From the remaining 903 we sample 100 as for CredData, by quartile of vulnerable-line count (cut points 2, 5, and 17; 31, 21, 24, and 24 slots) with a fixed seed. The selected instances have 1 to 411 vulnerable lines (median 5). As on CredData, every task is a different repository under the same fixed audit prompt, and each seed runs all 100. We grade strictly at the line level: a reported location counts only if its file and line match a line the fixing commit removed, and precision is taken over reported lines in the vulnerable files. Prior work instead reports binary F1 over whole functions or code samples on balanced sets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lekssays et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; under our metric, naming the right function but a different line scores zero. We keep the strict metric because it mirrors CredData’s line-level F1 and needs no further labels.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Task Stream Formulation and Cost Accounting</h2>

<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task-stream formalization.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3" title="3 TokenEconomizer ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> defines the task stream, the prefix <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the controller map <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 TokenEconomizer ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>; we reuse that notation here, writing <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}=F(S_{i-1},\tau_{i},\xi_{i},y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}=F(S_{i-1},\tau_{i},\xi_{i},y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for the state update. The trajectory alternates observations and agent actions, <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}=(o_{0},a_{0},o_{1},\ldots,a_{T},o_{T+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}=(o_{0},a_{0},o_{1},\ldots,a_{T},o_{T+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, so its turn count in <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 Meta-strategy III: Failure-loop Intervention ‣ 3 TokenEconomizer ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\xi_{i}|=T+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\xi_{i}|=T+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and its success label is <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is formed only from tasks preceding <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and the failure-loop guard observes only the prefix <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i,\leq t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i,\leq t}</annotation></semantics></math>, no decision for <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on a later task in the stream.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cost attribution and objective.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For task <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\mathrm{u}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\mathrm{u}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\mathrm{c}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\mathrm{c}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\mathrm{o}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\mathrm{o}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the uncached-input, cache-read, and output tokens consumed by the task agent. Let <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contain every auxiliary model call attributable to the task, including micro-skill proposal, tool judging, trace judging, and redirect writing. For each auxiliary call <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, we record the same three token classes. Controller overhead and total cache-aware cost are then</p>
<table id="A5.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A5.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E4X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}_{i}}\left(w_{u}N^{\mathrm{u}}_{i,h}+w_{c}N^{\mathrm{c}}_{i,h}+w_{o}N^{\mathrm{o}}_{i,h}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}_{i}}\left(w_{u}N^{\mathrm{u}}_{i,h}+w_{c}N^{\mathrm{c}}_{i,h}+w_{o}N^{\mathrm{o}}_{i,h}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.E4Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
