<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tool pruning.</h4>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A tool’s unused streak resets whenever it is called. Before each task, unprotected, never-called tools whose streak has reached three are masked; the other unprotected, never-called tools are sent to the judge, and those it marks irrelevant are masked too; if the judge call fails, they stay loaded. The mask is fixed for the whole task. A re-audit loads the full catalog without calling the judge.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Failure-loop intervention.</h4>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rule-based trigger keeps running counters of repeated commands and errors, edit churn, distinct edited files, absence of state change, and repeated verification failures, which the judge’s digest also reports. The judge is consulted only while the trigger is open, at most once every 5 turns; after each productive verdict, this interval grows by 5 turns, up to 20. A forced stop is checked before the judge and cannot be vetoed by it: once the trajectory’s turns, counted across a redirect, reach <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=2.5</annotation></semantics></math>, and the strict stall rule holds, the rollout ends without a further redirect. A failure signature is the first error line of a failing tool output, such as a traceback, a failed test, or an assertion, with numbers, memory addresses, and file paths removed, so that two outputs that fail the same way match. The strict stall rule holds when three conditions hold at once: edit churn, with at least 4 edits to one file or edits to at least 3 files; dense error output, with at least 6 error outputs; and repetition, with at least 20% of failing outputs repeating an earlier failure signature. The budget in <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 Meta-strategy III: Failure-loop Intervention ‣ 3 TokenEconomizer ‣ TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> uses a high percentile of passed turn counts rather than the maximum, so one unusually long success cannot inflate it, while repositories whose successful runs are typically long still receive a proportionally larger budget. The redirect is an LLM-authored summary of the agent’s own actions that asks it to try a materially different hypothesis.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Prompt Templates</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix gives the prompts of the controller’s model calls, along with the prefix that micro-skill injection adds to the agent’s prompt. Slots filled at run time are shown in braces.</p>
</div>
